## Context

The `.aide` suffix was chosen because it is distinctive, memorable, and
has no collisions with existing formats — but the same properties mean
no editor recognizes it out of the box. Without IDE support, `.aide`
files render as plain unstyled text, and the whole convention starts to
look like a hack. This module's job is to make the suffix visually
first-class in every editor the project supports, using whatever
mechanism each editor actually allows.

The research that led to the Zed-vs-VS-Code split is load-bearing. The
resulting decisions — "no Zed extension, yes VS Code extension" — are
counterintuitive enough that without the reasoning on file the
question gets re-litigated every time someone new looks at the folder.
That reasoning lives in the Strategy section below so it stays next to
the code it justifies.

## Strategy

**VS Code ships as a bundled extension because its API supports exactly
the mapping we need.** VS Code lets an extension declare that a new
file suffix should be handled by an existing built-in language.
Declaring `.aide` as belonging to the editor's built-in Markdown
language is enough: the built-in grammar, highlighting, and preview
pane all apply automatically, with no grammar bundled and no
maintenance cost as the editor's Markdown support evolves. The packaged
`.vsix` is checked into the repo (under `extensions/vscode/`) so the
helper can install it locally without touching the marketplace, and
the same package works for every VS Code fork that speaks the same
extension API — Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot-in-VSCode.

**Zed ships as a config patch because its extension API cannot produce
a preview pane.** Zed's Markdown preview is a hardcoded built-in view
gated on a file's language being the built-in Markdown language — not
on the file's grammar, not on anything an extension can influence.
Zed's extension API exposes grammars, language servers, themes, and
slash commands, but does not expose custom rendered views. No
extension, no matter how it is shaped, can produce a preview pane for
a non-Markdown language. We verified this the hard way: every extension
shape we tried — custom language pointed at the Markdown grammar,
bundled query files copied from Zed's own Markdown support, reusing
the Markdown grammar by name — produced the same outcome. Highlighting
ranged from broken to partial to working, and the preview button never
appeared in any configuration, because preview is gated on language
identity, not on grammar. The only path to rendered preview is the
user-settings file-type mapping, which tells Zed to treat `.aide`
files **as Markdown files** — same language, same preview, same
everything. configureZed merges this setting into
`.zed/settings.json` at init time, and there is no Zed extension to
publish, maintain, or ship.

**Per-editor strategies beat cross-editor consistency.** The instinct
to ship one extension per editor in a consistent shape is wrong here.
Zed and VS Code have genuinely different extensibility models, and a
consistent shape would force either a broken Zed extension or a
VS-Code-as-settings-patch approach that throws away the parts of the
VS Code extension API that actually work. The right answer is to
match each editor's real capability and let init handle the
divergence — the developer experience is identical across editors
even though the mechanism underneath is not.

**Everything ships in-repo, nothing ships over the network.** The
`.vsix` is checked in so init on a freshly-cloned machine can install
it without a network call, a marketplace account, or a build step.
New versions bump the filename, so the helper can pick the latest and
install idempotently. Publishing to a marketplace is a maintainer
activity, not an end-user activity, and this helper must never depend
on it.

**Preserve the user's config, always.** Both helpers read the existing
file first, patch only the specific keys they care about, and write
back. A parse failure on `.zed/settings.json` is a skip, not a
rewrite — the user's config is not something we are allowed to
clobber when we cannot understand it.

## Good examples

A developer clones a project and runs the install action — through
either the `aide_init` MCP tool or the npx CLI — and the install
service calls configureZed and configureVscode as part of the IDE
category. The helpers report:

> Zed config: Created
> VS Code extension: Installed

They open a `.aide` file in Zed: full syntax highlighting, the
preview pane opens, headings and lists render the same as any `.md`
file in the project. They open the same file in Cursor: full syntax
highlighting, the preview pane opens, same result. Two editors, two
mechanisms underneath, one consistent experience from the developer's
seat.

A developer with a heavily customized `.zed/settings.json` re-runs
the install a second time. configureZed reads the existing file,
sees that the Markdown file-type mapping already includes `*.aide`,
and writes nothing. Their custom theme, keybindings, and language
settings are all untouched, and the helper reports `exists`.

A developer without the `code` CLI on PATH runs the install action.
configureVscode tries `code --version`, catches the failure, and
reports `skipped`. Every other category in the install continues to
run, and the developer is never blocked from adopting AIDE just
because they do not use VS Code.

## Bad examples

A Zed extension shipped alongside the VS Code one "for consistency".
The developer installs it, opens a `.aide` file, and sees partially
working highlighting but no preview button anywhere — not in the
command palette, not on right-click, not on any shortcut. Their
conclusion is that either the extension is half-finished or the AIDE
convention itself is amateur, and they stop using `.aide` files. The
extension technically installed and technically did something, and
that is exactly why it is worse than shipping no Zed extension.

A VS Code extension that ships its own Markdown grammar and registers
`.aide` as a brand-new language. Highlighting looks almost right but
subtly lags the editor's built-in Markdown — a new Markdown feature
lands in VS Code and `.aide` files do not get it. The preview shortcut
does nothing, because the built-in preview is bound to the built-in
Markdown language, not to the custom one. Every friction point here is
self-inflicted: the editor offered a zero-cost mapping to its own
Markdown language and the extension declined it.

A configureZed that encounters an unparseable `.zed/settings.json` and
responds by writing a fresh one from a template. The user's themes,
keybindings, and language settings are gone, and they find out by
reading a diff they did not expect.

A configureVscode that errors the whole install run because `code`
is not on PATH. The developer does not use VS Code, never wanted
the extension, and has now been blocked from writing methodology,
scaffolding commands, and wiring MCP by a check they had no reason
to care about — regardless of whether the install was invoked via
the MCP tool or the CLI.
